[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: StrictDoc tooling for this repository

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-FINGERPRINT-IN-NODE
TITLE: Store the parent contract fingerprint in the node
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A second consumer needs per-edge state that a node field cannot carry, such as
a reviewer identity or a waiver with its own expiry.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
A node records the contract hash of each parent it depends on, in a PARENT_FP
field on the node itself, rather than in a sidecar file keyed by node identity.
Fingerprints point strictly downward: DECISION to MECHANISM to SLICE.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Accepting a change becomes a reviewable git diff, attributable to a commit and
revertable. A sidecar makes sign-off invisible and needs a key-matching scheme.
The downward-only rule means a garbage-collectable node has no inbound
fingerprint, so deleting it can never strand one.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY
TITLE: Implementability is a property of a closure, not of the project
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A release needs a single project-wide readiness answer, which no per-slice
query can supply.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
A slice is implementable when its own transitive contract-dependency closure is
settled and carries no suspect edges. There is no global readiness gate. The
count of open decisions in that closure is the slice's degrees of freedom, and
handoff is that count reaching zero.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A global fail-closed gate is waterfall by construction: nothing is implementable
until everything is designed. A closure-local predicate lets a mature slice ship
while the rest of the graph stays deliberately fuzzy.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-DEFER-LAYER
TITLE: Omit the LAYER node type until a corpus with layers arrives
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The first document whose mechanisms sit in distinct architectural layers, at
which point the cast tables' locus column becomes the LAYER assignment.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The grammar ships without LAYER. Horizontal position is therefore unmodelled;
only the vertical axis, a SLICE and the mechanisms it Crosses, is expressible.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
No content in this document sits in a layer. Adding LAYER now would make every
MECHANISM carry a field with one possible value.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
This is a real gap, not a cost-free deferral: half the horizontal-and-vertical
tracking the operator asked for is absent until it is closed.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-CHECK
TITLE: Suspect-link detection over the JSON export
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Hash each node's contract-bearing fields from the JSON export. A node is suspect
when a parent it records a fingerprint for no longer hashes to the recorded
value, or when that parent has been deleted. Excludes RATIONALE and NOTES.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
StrictDoc has no suspect-link feature. It is on the upstream backlog as
SDOC-SRS-117 and described unbuilt in their DO-178 notes.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-FINGERPRINT-IN-NODE:0000000
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-FINGERPRINT-IN-NODE
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-CYCLE-CHECK
TITLE: Cycle detection over the JSON export
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Detect relation cycles in the exported graph independently of StrictDoc.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
StrictDoc's own cycle detection queries the graph without specifying an edge
role, and the graph database returns only the bucket of role-less edges. Every
relation in this grammar carries a role, so no cycle in this corpus is detected;
the failure surfaces as a recursion-depth crash during render.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY:0000000
NOTES: >>>
Implemented: dev/scripts/cycle-check.py, an iterative (non-recursive, so it
cannot hit the exact recursion-depth failure it replaces) DFS over every
Parent-type RELATIONS entry regardless of ROLE -- File relations excluded,
since their VALUE names a filesystem path, not a node. CI gate:
checks/strictdoc-cycle-check.nix, mirroring strictdoc-fp-check.nix's clean
$TMPDIR export harness (SLICE-FP-DETECTOR) rather than sharing its
derivation, so the two checks stay independently attributable on failure.

Verified past the happy path with two synthetic fixtures, not just the real
graph: a 3-node cycle where every edge carries a role (A --Assumes--> B
--Assumes--> C --Assumes--> A) is caught and reported with its role chain,
plus a disjoint acyclic pair in the same export to prove no cross-node false
positive; and an acyclic diamond (two paths converging on one node) plus a
File relation whose VALUE equals its own owning node's UID, to prove that
is not mistaken for a self-loop. Real graph: 61 nodes, 36 role-carrying
edges, 0 cycles found.

Positive control re-run independently at review, against the real graph rather than a fixture: a role-carrying two-cycle was injected by closing a loop back against an existing Governed_By edge. strictdoc export exited 0 and reported nothing; cycle-check exited 1 and named the path. The role-blind premise is therefore measured here, not inherited.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-READY-QUERY
TITLE: Ready-slice query over the contract closure
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
One function in dev/scripts/sdoc_fp.py walks a node's contract-dependency closure from the JSON export and returns a verdict record. dev/scripts/ready.py is its command line, beside cycle-check.py and fp-check.py. The closure follows every relation whose TYPE is Parent, regardless of role, and every PARENT_FP entry. The filter is on Parent rather than on not-File: a Child relation points downstream and is not a dependency, and a complement filter would walk one into the closure the day the grammar declares one.

The record carries, for one root: the root's own DEPTH or STATUS, held separate from its dependencies; whether every dependency is settled; the frontier, meaning the unsettled dependencies that have no unsettled dependency of their own, each with its hop distance from the root and the reason it blocks; how many unsettled nodes sit behind that frontier; the closure size; the open decisions in the closure, which are the degrees of freedom; and the suspect fingerprint entries whose node falls inside the closure, taken from fp-check's finding list rather than recomputed here.

Ready means every dependency is settled and the root itself is settled. What settled MEANS -- which DEPTH values qualify, and whether the cut differs by node type -- is not decided here: it is the single predicate MECH-READINESS-PREDICATE-RECONCILE owns, imported and never restated. A settled closure under an unsettled root is ready to design, which is a different action from ready to implement, and the record says which. The root never appears in its own blocker list.

ready.py prints the record as text, --json emits it verbatim, and --packet prints the closure through docs/sdoc/render.py in dependency-first order as the bounded brief handed to an implementing session. With no root it reports every SLICE. It exits 0 whenever the export parsed; --require-ready opts a caller into exit 1 on a blocked root.

Consumers: docs/sdoc/status.py drops its own closure_verdict and calls the shared function; fp-accept's readiness refusal calls the same predicate; the implementation-session prompt in docs/sdoc/README.md is replaced by --packet; and the milestone-four checkpoint wiring registers the record as gate evidence. The function is the interface and the script is one front end, so the tool from SLICE-SDOC-CLI imports it rather than reimplementing it.

Out of scope, deliberately. This node owns the traversal, the record and the report, not the rule: what settled means for a rejected, superseded or suspect parent is MECH-READINESS-PREDICATE-RECONCILE's single predicate, imported here and never restated. Nothing here writes, signs, or fails nix flake check.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The query already exists informally and disagrees with itself. status.py's closure_verdict is one reading, sdoc_fp.is_ready another, and neither applies the no-suspect-edge clause the definition names. One function returning one record is what stops a fourth reading appearing when the milestone-two tool and the milestone-three behaviour model each need the same answer; a third standalone script would be the drift INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE names.

It reports and does not gate. DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY refuses a global readiness gate, and the one time the graph said blocked the operator directed implementation anyway and it shipped clean, so a blocked verdict is information for whoever is deciding, not a refusal.

The record is specified as data with a text rendering rather than as printed output because a gate in milestone four has to consume the verdict, and parsing a report back out of a terminal is how the fifth definition of ready gets written.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY:0000000
NOTES: >>>
Measured against the current export, 139 nodes, on 2026-08-27.

Closures are small enough to be a prompt. The deepest is SLICE-INSTANCE-SEMANTICS-MIGRATION at 34 nodes and 59,740 characters of field text, against 232,966 for the whole graph rendered by render.py --all. The packet is a quarter of the corpus, not a tenth, and that is the worst case in the graph today.

The frontier is a weaker filter than MECH-READINESS-REPORT-FRONTIER assumes. On that same slice, 21 unsettled dependencies excluding the root, of which 16 are in the frontier -- 10 of the 21 are sketch nodes with no dependencies at all, so they qualify by definition. The cut that actually shortens the report is hop distance: 5 of the 21 are direct dependencies and the closure is 4 hops deep. Hence both axes in the record. That node's "twenty-three blockers" measures 22 today including the root.

status.py does not apply the no-suspect-edge clause, measured rather than read. Replacing MECH-SEMBLE-EXTRA-GRAMMARS's PARENT_FP hash with a wrong non-placeholder value in a scratch copy of the export makes fp-check exit 1 reporting one suspect entry, while status.py's verdict for SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE, whose closure contains that node, is unchanged. Positive control on the same node and the same copy: setting its DEPTH to sketch does change the verdict, which prints the new blocker.

All 31 fingerprint entries currently have a matching relation, 0 unbacked, so the relation set and the PARENT_FP set coincide. MECH-FP-RELATION-LINT is a check and not a write-time invariant, so the walk still unions them; ignoring the fingerprint half is how an earlier status.py reported a blocked slice as ready.

Degrees of freedom is not a headline on its own. Seven of the twelve slices are blocked with zero open decisions in their closure, because unsettled mechanisms do not move the count; only SLICE-CHECKPOINT-WIRING at 1 and SLICE-INSTANCE-SEMANTICS-MIGRATION at 2 have any.

MECH-READY-QUERY-GRAPH-REUSE is answered: cycle-check.py's build_graph is this walk minus PARENT_FP, so it moves into sdoc_fp.py with a flag for whether fingerprint parents count as edges.

MECH-READINESS-REPORT-FRONTIER is subsumed. Both of its complaints -- the root reporting itself as its own blocker, and an undifferentiated blocker list -- are answered by this record, so it closes when this lands rather than being groomed separately.

LEFT TO THE OPERATOR, deliberately. This milestone settles the plumbing;
each of these is a rule about how this instance works and milestone five is
where they get written.

- What settled means for a rejected, superseded or suspect parent. Deliberately not decided here; it is MECH-READINESS-PREDICATE-RECONCILE's single predicate, and this node consumes it as a parameter.

- Whether the closure stops at a settled node or walks through it into that node's own unsettled dependencies. Live instance: SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE is verified yet reports blocked, because it crosses MECH-SEMBLE-EXTRA-GRAMMARS (implemented) whose Governed_By parent DEC-ARCH-COLOCATION is open. Filed as a backlog DECISION.

- Whether relation roles weigh differently in the closure. The walk treats Governed_By, Crosses, Assumes, Proven_By and Guarantees identically; whether an Assumes edge should block as hard as a Crosses edge is a semantic call, filed as a backlog DECISION.

- Whether readiness ever becomes a merge or commit gate. Specified here as advisory with an opt-in --require-ready flag, so milestone four can arm it without this node choosing the policy.

- Which DEPTH values count as settled, and whether the cut differs per node type. DEPTH is instance-layer by MECH-SEMANTICS-DEFINITION-BOUNDARY's own wording, so this node declares the traversal and imports the cut rather than fixing it.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY
  ROLE: Governed_By

[SPIKE]
UID: SPIKE-SGRA-PARSE
TITLE: Make the tree-sitter grammar parse standalone .sgra files
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: run
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A three-rule patch is applied and the two .sgra files parse with no regression
across the sdoc corpus.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The upstream tree-sitter grammar parses all sdoc documents but fails every
standalone .sgra grammar file, because its root rule requires a DOCUMENT header.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Run. The DOCUMENT-header fix alone was not sufficient: our own grammar.sgra
also uses REVERSE_ROLE on every non-File Parent relation, and upstream's
grammar_relation_parent/child rules had no field for it at all, so it still
errored after the root-rule fix. The applied patch is therefore three rule
changes across two files (source_file's root choice, a new standalone_grammar
rule, and REVERSE_ROLE added to both relation rules) rather than one.

Measured against this repo's own corpus (6 .sdoc + grammar.sgra): 6/7 clean
before the patch, 7/7 after. Measured against strictdoc-project/strictdoc's
own docs/ (15 files, pinned rev 215cda2e): 5/15 clean both before and after —
the other 10 fail on pre-existing document-body/FREETEXT coverage gaps this
patch never targeted (out of DEC-GRAMMAR-PATCH-NOT-FORK's scope; logged as
MECH-SGRA-BODY-COVERAGE in the plan backlog). checks/strictdoc-grammar-corpus.nix
is the standing regression gate against both corpora.
<<<

